<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/foundational/form-follows-forces">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Architecture begins with a structural decision disguised as a shape decision. A
    <strong>beam</strong> carries load by <em>bending</em> — one face stretches, the other squeezes,
    and the middle does expensive work. An <strong>arch</strong> carries the same load by
    <em>pure compression</em> — every stone pushing on its neighbour, no pulling anywhere. Stone is
    strong in compression and weak in tension, which is why the Romans, who had no steel, built arches
    that still stand two thousand years later (the Pont du Gard aqueduct carries water across a river
    on three tiers of them). Every building technology since is a position on this map: which loads,
    carried which way, in which material — and the architect's technical knowledge is, at bottom,
    the skill of assessing those options against design intent, cost and performance before the
    concrete is poured. The buildings that survive their eras are usually the ones where shape and
    structure stopped fighting.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the hanging chain and the parabola — nearly identical shapes, subtly different physics" widget="plotter" config={archCfg} />

  <p>
    Flip the first curve upside down and you have the perfect arch: a chain hanging under its own
    weight traces a <strong>catenary</strong> (from Latin <em>catena</em>, chain — the cosh curve), and
    an arch following that shape carries pure compression with zero bending. The parabola is its
    cousin — correct when weight is spread evenly <em>along the span</em> (a bridge deck) rather than
    along the curve itself; the two shapes nearly coincide for shallow arches, which is why the
    difference confuses even engineers. Robert Hooke described the trick in 1675 (as an anagram he
    guarded until his death, to keep the priority): "as hangs the flexible line, so but inverted will
    stand the rigid arch." Antoni Gaudí built design instruments on it — hanging weighted chains
    upside down for the Colònia Güell church, letting gravity itself compute the compression-only
    geometry, a method he applied across his Barcelona works including the Sagrada Família. The
    Gateway Arch in St Louis (1965) is the same idea at monument scale — a flattened, weighted
    catenary in stainless steel, standing because every section pushes on the next.
  </p>

  <h2>Established systems: the load-bearing canon</h2>
  <p>
    The established technologies form a short canon, each an answer to "how does load reach the
    ground?" <strong>Load-bearing masonry</strong> — walls that are the structure — governed
    building for five millennia; its constraint is that openings weaken walls and height is limited
    by the mortar's crushing strength, so it produces thick, small-windowed architecture.
    <strong>The arch, vault and dome</strong> escape the lintel's span limit: a barrel vault is a
    continuous arch; a dome is an arch rotated — the Pantheon's 43-metre unreinforced concrete dome
    (c. 126 CE) remains the world's largest, its builders grading the concrete from heavy basalt at
    the base to featherweight pumice at the top, an early exercise in exactly the material
    optimisation this subject teaches. <strong>Gothic structural skeletons</strong> (12th–13th
    centuries) refined the arch into pointed ribs and <strong>flying buttresses</strong> — external
    struts that catch the sideways thrust of vaults and deliver it to the ground — turning stone
    walls from structure into curtain, which is why cathedrals could dissolve into glass.
    <strong>Post-and-beam frames</strong> are the other great family (timber framing, classical
    colonnades, and their modern descendants), carrying load through bending members rather than
    compression arcs. The 1880s broke the height barrier with two paired inventions: the
    <strong>safety elevator</strong> (Otis, 1850s) and the <strong>steel skeleton frame</strong>
    (Chicago, Home Insurance Building, 1885) — a material strong in tension meant beams could span
    far and frames could stack floor upon floor, and the skyscraper, with its curtain wall hung off
    the structure rather than bearing on it, became possible. <strong>Reinforced concrete</strong>
    (Hennebique's system, 1890s) merged both material virtues in one composite: concrete carries
    compression, steel reinforcing bars (rebar) carry tension — the two are partners because they
    expand at nearly the same rate with heat, and the alkaline concrete protects the steel from
    rust. Nearly every building you have ever entered is some assembly of these: slabs transfer to
    beams, beams to columns or walls, walls to foundations, foundations to ground — the
    <strong>load path</strong>, the single idea every structural drawing is secretly about.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the arch does not "hold" by mortar gluing stones together —
  it holds by geometry. Cut the mortar away and a well-built arch can still stand, each wedge-shaped
  voussoir locked by compression; what it cannot survive is having its shape altered, because the
  shape <em>is</em> the load path. Architects say form follows forces because, in compression
  structures, the sentence is literal.</p>

  <h2>Assessing technologies: design, economics, performance</h2>
  <p>
    Knowing the systems is half the syllabus; the other half is <em>choosing</em> among them, and
    honest assessment runs three checks. <strong>Design fit</strong>: does the technology serve the
    building's intent? Span and grid set the first test (a column-free auditorium demands long spans
    — steel trusses or concrete shells — while a laboratory tolerates tight grids); services
    integration, future flexibility and even acoustics follow. The 20th-century modernists built the
    aesthetic case: Sullivan's dictum that "form ever follows function" and Mies van der Rohe's
    "less is more" were, structurally, arguments that the honest expression of the load path
    <em>is</em> the architecture — which is why exposed frames, expressed structure and visible
    services became a style rather than an accident. <strong>Economics</strong>: cost is structure
    plus time plus risk — material cost, but also the crane hours (prefabrication shifts labour from
    site, where weather and sequencing bleed money, to factory, where it is fast and repeatable),
    the foundation cost (steel's lightness can save more below ground than it spends above), and
    speed (each month of construction is interest and rent foregone). <strong>Performance over
    life</strong>: maintenance, durability, fire (steel loses strength dramatically near 500–600 °C
    and must be protected; concrete is naturally fire-resistant), thermal behaviour (masonry's mass
    smooths temperature swings; glass towers need engineering to avoid becoming greenhouses), and
    carbon — concrete's cement alone is responsible for roughly 7–8% of global CO<sub>2</sub>
    emissions, which has quietly made embodied carbon a first-order design criterion rather than a
    sustainability footnote. The professional skill is that these three checks trade against each
    other: the cheapest frame is rarely the fastest, the fastest is rarely the most flexible, and the
    assessment memo — not the shape model — is where architecture decides what it will be.
  </p>

  <h2>Emerging systems: what is changing now</h2>
  <p>
    The current generation of technologies is answering the carbon and speed checks.
    <strong>Mass timber</strong> — cross-laminated timber (CLT) panels and glulam beams, engineered
    so that layers glued crosswise cancel wood's direction-dependent weaknesses — now builds
    mid- and high-rise structures (the 85.4-metre Mjøstårnet in Norway, 2019, was the world's tallest
    timber building at completion) with a fraction of the embodied carbon of concrete, reviving
    post-and-beam logic with factory precision and, notably, wood's slight incombustibility advantage
    through charring rather than the steel-frame's conductivity problem. <strong>Prefabrication and
    modular construction</strong> push the factory logic to whole room-volumes stacked like bricks.
    <strong>3D-printed construction</strong> extrudes mortar layer by layer, eliminating formwork —
    the hidden cost inside every curved concrete shape, and the reason Candela's thin shells were
    heroic: Félix Candela's 1940s–50s hyperbolic paraboloid vaults achieved long spans in concrete
    centimetres thick by pure geometry, form resisting load the way the catenary does, at the cost of
    formwork carpenters could barely price. Digital formwork removal (printing) and computational
    form-finding (the hanging-chain model, now run as finite-element software) are making the
    compression-efficient shapes affordable for the first time since the Romans — shell structures
    and gridshells are returning, 3D-printed bridges included. <strong>Adaptive and low-carbon
    assemblies</strong> round the list: double-skin facades that ventilate themselves, phase-change
    materials that store heat, and the quiet revolution of <em>reusing</em> existing structure
    (retrofit-first design), because the lowest-carbon building is the one already standing.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading a building's load path</h2>
  <p>
    The load path is the single idea every structural drawing is secretly about, and you can read it
    in any building you enter. Take an ordinary house: the <strong>roof</strong> (a pair of rafters
    leaning on each other — a primitive arch, its spreading thrust caught by a ceiling-tie that
    works in tension, the member steel made easy); its weight lands on <strong>walls</strong>, which
    are load-bearing masonry or timber studs sheathed so they resist racking; the walls land on
    <strong>foundation footings</strong>, whose job is to spread the same weight over enough soil
    area that the ground beneath does not fail (soil, weak in everything but compression, is the
    ultimate bottom of every path). Now a modern office tower: glass panels carry only themselves
    (<em>curtain wall</em> — hung, like a sail, from the frame); floor <strong>slabs</strong> span to
    <strong>beams</strong>, beams to <strong>columns</strong> arranged on a regular grid, columns
    into a concrete core or pile caps, and down. The difference between the two buildings is not
    sophistication but <em>concentration</em>: the house spreads load through area (walls), the
    tower through lines (frames) — and every service, renovation or seismic retrofit begins by
    asking which members are on the path (untouchable) and which are mere infill (removable). This
    is the practical meaning of "assessing technologies": for the use you intend, which system
    carries the load with the least material, the least disruption, the least carbon — and the
    honest designer can trace every kilogram from its point of application to the earth.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three threads tie the canon to the frontier. <strong>The hierarchy of structural systems</strong>
    — load-bearing walls, frames, and surface-active structures (shells, membranes, tensegrity) — is
    a hierarchy of how load reaches the ground: through area (walls), through lines (beams and
    columns), or through curved surfaces carrying load in their skin. Surface-active systems are the
    most material-efficient because they eliminate bending — the expensive state — entirely, which
    is the deep reason the catenary is architecture's most honest shape and why Buckminster Fuller
    spent a career on geodesics and tensegrity (structures held stable by pure tension in cables and
    pure compression in floating struts). <strong>Assembly knowledge is the craft layer</strong>:
    between system and building sits the joint — how CLT panels connect, how a curtain wall
    thermally breaks from its slab, how the rebar laps at a column junction — and most real-world
    building failures (water ingress, thermal bridging, progressive collapse) are assembly failures,
    not system failures, which is why technical knowledge lives at the detail scale.
    <strong>Assessment is finally becoming quantitative:</strong> whole-building LCA (life-cycle
    assessment) software now prices the embodied carbon of a frame decision in tonnes of
    CO<sub>2</sub>-equivalent, and performance-based fire and seismic design lets engineers demonstrate
    safety by simulation rather than prescriptive code — the same shift the engineering subject
    describes as moving from safety factors to probabilistic limit states. Scrub the whole history
    once more and the through-line holds: Rome compressed, Gothic routed, Chicago framed, Hennebique
    partnered, Candela curved, and the present is decarbonising — each era a new agreement between
    form and force, and each agreement visible in the shape of the buildings it left standing.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hooke (1675); Allen & Zalewski, Form and Forces; Mainstone, Developments in Structural Form; EN 1990 Eurocode basis of design; Churton, mass timber and embodied carbon literature; Candela shell structure studies.</p>
